      "Short answer: **Valluru is *not* an eagle**, but **it belongs to the same broader family**.\n\nimage_group{\"query\":[\"Black kite Milvus migrans\",\"Brahminy kite Haliastur indus\",\"Indian eagle Aquila\",\"kite bird soaring India\"]}\n\n### Clear, correct classification\n- **Valluru (வல்லூரு)** = **Kite**\n  - Common species in Tamil Nadu: **Black Kite**\n  - Scientific name: *Milvus migrans*\n\n- **Family**: **Accipitridae**  \n  👉 This family includes **eagles, kites, hawks, and harriers**\n\n### So how to say it accurately\n- ❌ “Valluru is an eagle” → **Not correct**\n- ✅ “Valluru is from the *eagle family* (Accipitridae)” → **Correct**\n- Best phrasing:\n  > **Valluru is a kite, closely related to eagles, but not an eagle itself**\n\n### Simple way villagers vs science differ\n- **Village usage**: Big soaring bird = “eagle”\n- **Scientific usage**:\n  - Eagle = *Aquila* group (bigger, heavier, powerful talons)\n  - Kite = *Milvus / Haliastur* group (lighter, forked tail, gliding)\n\nIf you want, I can give:\n- 1-line Tamil explanation (for elders / kids)\n- Valluru vs Kalugu (eagle) comparison table\n- Cultural meaning of Valluru in Tamil lore"
